Good, now I can see the code. The `live-model-switch.ts` was checked out from `origin/main`. Now let me understand the issue.

In `resolveLiveSessionModelSelection`:
```javascript
const runtimeProvider = entry?.modelProvider?.trim();
const runtimeModel = entry?.model?.trim();
const provider = runtimeProvider || entry?.providerOverride?.trim() || defaultModelRef.provider;
const model = runtimeModel || entry?.modelOverride?.trim() || defaultModelRef.model;
```

So it uses `entry?.model` (which is the model's ID, separate from `providerOverride`), and falls back to `modelOverride`, then to `defaultModelRef`.


The problem is that `defaultModelRef` gets resolved through `resolveDefaultModelForAgent` when an agentId is provided, which pulls the agent's default model. For heartbeat and compaction runs, this would be the configured primary model (Sonnet), not the heartbeat-specific Haiku override. So when there's no explicit `modelOverride` in the session entry, it incorrectly falls back to the primary model instead of respecting the heartbeat's configuration.

The solution is to skip the model switch check entirely when we're in a heartbeat or compaction context, since those are configured operations that shouldn't trigger the same live session validation that applies to regular interactive sessions.

